##RISKS AND BEST PRACTICES
Selling ebooks online is relatively easy to start, but building a sustainable business requires attention to quality, copyright, customer satisfaction and platform policies.



Follow Copyright and Content Ownership Rules - 
Make sure the content you sell is original or that you have the necessary rights to use it.

Things to keep in mind:
Don't copy and sell someone else's ebook or paid material.
Don't use copyrighted images, illustrations or templates without permission or an appropriate licence.
Don't assume AI-generated content is automatically free from copyright issues.
Use properly licensed images and resources.
Keep records of licences and permissions where necessary.
Don't present someone else's work as your own.



Review AI-Generated Content Carefully - 
AI tools can help with research, writing and editing, but they can also produce inaccurate or misleading information.

Best practices:
Verify facts, statistics and references.
Rewrite robotic or repetitive sections.
Add useful examples and practical explanations.
Don't invent personal experiences, testimonials or results.
Make sure the final ebook provides genuine value.
Follow the relevant AI tool's terms of use.



Maintain Ebook Quality - 
Your ebook represents your brand, so make sure it is worth the price customers are paying.

Before publishing, check:
Grammar, spelling and formatting.
Accuracy of information.
Page layout and readability.
Working links and references.
Whether the ebook delivers the promise made in its description.
Whether the PDF opens properly on different devices.



Follow Platform Policies - 
Your business depends on platforms such as Threads, Instagram and Gumroad. Violating their policies can affect your account or product availability.

Best practices:
Follow Threads and Instagram Community Guidelines.
Review Gumroad's prohibited products and content policies.
Avoid spam, misleading promotions and deceptive marketing.
Don't use fake engagement or purchased followers.
Keep your account and payment information secure.
Check platform policies regularly because they can change.



Be Honest With Customers - 
Trust is important when selling digital products. Customers should clearly understand what they are purchasing.

Things to keep in mind:
Write an accurate product description.
Show genuine previews of your ebook.
Clearly mention what is included.
Avoid unrealistic promises or guaranteed results.
Don't use fake reviews or testimonials.
Respond professionally to customer questions and complaints.



Understand Refunds and Payment Issues - 
Digital product sales can involve refunds, failed payments and payment disputes.

Best practices:
Clearly communicate your refund policy before purchase.
Follow the refund and dispute rules of your selling platform.
Keep records of transactions and customer communications.
Make sure customers receive the correct download.
Never promise that a refund is impossible if platform rules or applicable consumer laws provide otherwise.



Protect Your Business and Accounts - 
Your social media and storefront accounts are important business assets.

Best practices:
Use strong, unique passwords.
Enable two-factor authentication.
Secure your recovery email and phone number.
Never share OTPs or login credentials.
Be careful with suspicious links and messages.
Keep backup copies of your ebooks and important business records.



Manage Your Finances Properly - 
Revenue is not the same as profit. Track your earnings and expenses to understand whether your business is actually profitable.

Keep records of:
Total ebook sales.
Platform and payment processing fees.
Marketing and advertising expenses.
Freelancers and software subscriptions.
Refunds and other business expenses.
Final profit and applicable taxes.



Things to Keep in Mind - 
Never sacrifice quality for quick sales.
Don't make claims you cannot support.
Protect your original work and respect others' intellectual property.
Follow applicable platform rules and laws.
Build customer trust through transparency.
Treat ebook selling as a real business, not a guaranteed source of income.
